{"term":"particle","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:c066a3fe7fbcf3a197","definition":"A very small piece of matter, a fragment; especially, the smallest possible part of something.","example":"Be content with the knowledge that, ere the voyage had ended, both she and I were desperately and unreasoningly in love with one another. Heaven knows that I can make the admission now without one particle of vanity.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["bit","chip","chunk","tranche","clip","crumb","crumbling","cutting","flake","fleak","fragment","hunk","lump","mammock","ort","piece","piecemeal","portion","scrap","scraplet","slice","snead","snippet","steck"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["adparticle","adverbial particle","alpha particle","angel particle","antiparticle","anti-particle","astroparticle","beta particle","bioparticle","cascade particle","charged particle","charged-particle","composite particle","cosmoparticle","delta particle","Dirac particle","ferroparticle","fundamental particle","gamma particle","geoparticle","God particle","gravitationally interacting massive particle","Higgs particle","Hitler particle","holoparticle","hot particle","immunoparticle","infraparticle","interparticle","intraparticle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/particle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:775cebac4ff2a7f27d","definition":"Any of various physical objects making up the constituent parts of an atom; an elementary particle or subatomic particle.","example":"What, he asked himself, does quantum theory have to say about the familiar properties of particles such as position?","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["adparticle","adverbial particle","alpha particle","angel particle","antiparticle","anti-particle","astroparticle","beta particle","bioparticle","cascade particle","charged particle","charged-particle","composite particle","cosmoparticle","delta particle","Dirac particle","ferroparticle","fundamental particle","gamma particle","geoparticle","God particle","gravitationally interacting massive particle","Higgs particle","Hitler particle","holoparticle","hot particle","immunoparticle","infraparticle","interparticle","intraparticle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/particle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:f583bd212d2961135e","definition":"A part of speech that has no inherent lexical definition but must be associated with another word to impart meaning, often a grammatical category: for example, the English word to in a full infinitive phrase (to eat) or O in a vocative phrase (O Canada), or as a discourse marker (mmm).","example":"In English there is no grammatical device to differentiate predicational judgments from nonpredicational descriptions. This distinction does cast a shadow on the grammatical sphere to some extent, but recognition of it must generally be made in semantic terms. It is maintained here that in Japanese, on the other hand, the distinction is grammatically realized through the use of the two particles wa and ga.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["p-word"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["adparticle","adverbial particle","alpha particle","angel particle","antiparticle","anti-particle","astroparticle","beta particle","bioparticle","cascade particle","charged particle","charged-particle","composite particle","cosmoparticle","delta particle","Dirac particle","ferroparticle","fundamental particle","gamma particle","geoparticle","God particle","gravitationally interacting massive particle","Higgs particle","Hitler particle","holoparticle","hot particle","immunoparticle","infraparticle","interparticle","intraparticle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/particle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:0b6c9e924b1c807c27","definition":"A part of speech which cannot be inflected.","example":"322. The parts of speech which are neither declined nor conjugated, are called by the general name of particles. 323. They are adverbs, prepositions, conjunctions, and interjections.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["adparticle","adverbial particle","alpha particle","angel particle","antiparticle","anti-particle","astroparticle","beta particle","bioparticle","cascade particle","charged particle","charged-particle","composite particle","cosmoparticle","delta particle","Dirac particle","ferroparticle","fundamental particle","gamma particle","geoparticle","God particle","gravitationally interacting massive particle","Higgs particle","Hitler particle","holoparticle","hot particle","immunoparticle","infraparticle","interparticle","intraparticle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/particle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:b3f27d17b6364c2e6d","definition":"In the Roman Catholic church, a crumb of consecrated bread; also the smaller breads used in the communion of the laity.","example":"the Sacred Particle","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["adparticle","adverbial particle","alpha particle","angel particle","antiparticle","anti-particle","astroparticle","beta particle","bioparticle","cascade particle","charged particle","charged-particle","composite particle","cosmoparticle","delta particle","Dirac particle","ferroparticle","fundamental particle","gamma particle","geoparticle","God particle","gravitationally interacting massive particle","Higgs particle","Hitler particle","holoparticle","hot particle","immunop